These research areas contribute to developing robust active visual perception systems, facilitating improved environmental interaction and navigation in complex, dynamic settings. We briefly introduce these three components and summarize the methods mentioned in each part in Table IV.
1) Visual Simultaneous Localisation and Mapping: Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) is a technique that determines a mobile robot’s position in an unknown environment while concurrently constructing a map of that environment [121], [122]. Range-based SLAM [123]–[125] creates point cloud representations using rangefinders (e.g., laser scanners, radar, and/or sonar), but is costly and provides limited environmental information. Visual SLAM (vSLAM) [118], [119] uses on-board cameras to capture frames and construct a representation of the environment. It has gained popularity due to its low hardware cost, high accuracy in small-scale scenarios, and ability to capture rich environmental information. Classical vSLAM techniques can be divided into Traditional vSLAM and Semantic vSLAM [119].
Traditional vSLAM systems estimate the robot’s pose in an unknown environment using image information and multiview geometry principles to construct a low-level map (e.g., sparse maps, semi-dense maps, and dense maps) composed of point clouds, such as filter-based methods (e.g., MonoSLAM [75], MSCKF [76]), keyframe-based methods (e.g., PTAM [77], ORB-SLAM [78]), and direct tracking methods (e.g., DTAM [79], LSD-SLAM [80]). Since point clouds in lowlevel maps do not correspond directly to objects in the environment, making them difficult for embodied robots to interpret and utilize. However, the advent of semantic concepts, particularly semantic vSLAM systems integrated with seman


 PREPRINT 8
tic information solutions, has significantly improved robots’ ability to perceive and navigate unexplored environments. Early works, such as SLAM++ [81], use real-time 3D object recognition and tracking to create efficient object graphs, enabling robust loop closure, relocalization, and object detection in cluttered environments. CubeSLAM [82] and HDP-SLAM [83] introduce 3-D rectangular into the map to construct a lightweight semantic map. QuadricSLAM [84] employs semantic 3D ellipsoids to achieve precise modeling of object shapes and poses in complex geometrical environments. So-SLAM [85] incorporates fully coupled spatial structure constraints (coplanarity, collinearity, and proximity) in indoor environments. To meet the challenges of dynamic environments, DS-SLAM [86], DynaSLAM [87] and SG-SLAM [88] employ semantic segmentation for motion consistency checks and multiview geometry algorithms to identify and filter dynamic objects, ensuring stable localization and mapping. OVD-SLAM [89] leverages semantic, depth, and optical flow information to distinguish dynamic regions without predefined labels, achieving more accurate and robust localization. GSSLAM [90] utilizes 3D Gaussian representation that balances efficiency and accuracy through a real-time differentiable splatting rendering pipeline and adaptive expansion strategy. 2) 3D Scene Understanding: 3D scene understanding aims to distinguish objects’ semantics, identify their locations, and infer the geometric attributes from 3D scene data, which is fundamental in autonomous driving [126], robot navigation [127], and human-computer interaction [128] etc. A scene may be recorded as 3D point clouds using 3D scanning tools like LiDAR or RGB-D sensors. Unlike images, point clouds are sparse, disordered, and irregular, [120] makes scene interpretation extremely challenging. In recent years, numerous deep learning methods for 3D scene understanding have been proposed, which can be divided into projection-based, voxel-based, and point-based methods. Concretely, projection-based methods (e.g., MV3D [91], PointPillars [92], MVCNN [93] ) project 3D points onto various image planes and employ 2D CNN-based backbones for feature extraction. Voxel-based methods convert point clouds into regular voxel grids to facilitate 3D convolution operations (e.g., VoxNet [94], SSCNet [95]), and some works improve their efficiency through sparse convolution (e.g., MinkowskiNet [96], SSCNs [97], Embodiedscan [98]). In contrast, point-based methods process point clouds directly (e.g., PointNet [99], PointNet++ [100], PointMLP [101]). Recently, to achieve model scalability, Transformers-based (e.g., PointTransformer [102], Swin3d [103], PT2 [104], PT3 [105], 3D-VisTA [106], LEO [107], PQ3D [108]) and Mambabased (e.g., PointMamba [109], PCM [110], Mamba3D [111]) architectures have emerged. It is worth noting that in addition to directly using features from point clouds, PQ3D [108] also seamlessly combines features from multi-view images and voxels to enhance scene understanding capabilities. 3) Active Exploration: The previously introduced 3D scene understanding methods endow robots with the ability to perceive the environment in a passive manner. In such cases, the perception system’s information acquisition and decisionmaking do not adapt to the evolving scene. However, passive
